  "Question": "The number of real values of \\(x\\) that satisfy the equation\n\n\\[ (2^{6x+3})(4^{3x+6})=8^{4x+5} \\]\n\nis:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "greater than \\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Since \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(8=2^3\\), rewriting all powers with base \\(2\\) lets us compare the two sides directly. All bases are positive, so these powers and the usual exponent laws are valid for every real \\(x\\).\n\nThe left side becomes\n\\[\n2^{6x+3}4^{3x+6}\n=2^{6x+3}2^{6x+12}\n=2^{12x+15}.\n\\]\nThe right side becomes\n\\[\n8^{4x+5}=2^{3(4x+5)}=2^{12x+15}.\n\\]\nThus the two sides are equal for every real \\(x\\), with no excluded inputs. There are \\(\\boxed{\\text{infinitely many}}\\) real solutions, which is greater than \\(3\\), choice E.",
